>>
endobj
437 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 134 0 R
/T (6.1 Three Recurring Regulatory Challenge Themes)
/E (6.1 Three Recurring Regulatory Challenge Themes)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
438 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (In line with the Lot 1 mandate, this study identifies the following three recurring regulatory challenge themes across both the survey data and the interview narratives:)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
439 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1015 0 R
/E (Theme 1: Cross-border data governance fragmentation. Startups attempting to scale across African borders consistently encounter conflicting data protection requirements that require costly technical redesign, slow expansion timelines, and increase operational costs. This challenge affects all four sectors and every country pair examined in the interview cohort. It cannot be resolved at the individual startup level, it requires harmonised regional frameworks.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
440 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1016 0 R
/E (Theme 2: Regulatory gaps around emerging commercial models. Startups in carbon markets, digital traceability, peer-to-peer energy trading, and AI-assisted food safety are commercially ready to launch products that existing regulatory frameworks do not adequately govern. The result is not prohibition but legal uncertainty which is commercially equivalent. Startups choose not to proceed rather than risk undefined liability.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
441 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1017 0 R
/E (Theme 3: Government data inaccessibility. Across all four sectors, the data most needed by startups: malnutrition statistics, grid load data, soil fertility maps, emissions records, utility usage data, is held by governments and utilities that lack the open data infrastructure, API frameworks, or policy mandates to share it. This is the most fundamental structural barrier to AI adoption in this cohort.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
442 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 134 0 R
/T (6.2 Key Conclusions)
/E (6.2 Key Conclusions)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
443 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1018 0 R
/E (Data is mission-critical, but the access infrastructure does not match the need. The most commonly cited data access barrier is poor data quality, affecting 59% of respondents. Government and utility data that startups in agri-food and clean energy sectors depend on remains largely inaccessible, forcing reliance on expensive third-party sources or fragmented self-collected data.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
444 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1019 0 R
/E (AI adoption is real and growing, but the constraints are infrastructural and financial rather than primarily legal. Infrastructure cost, output reliability, and customer market readiness are the dominant AI barriers in this cohort. Legal templates will help, but cannot substitute for investment in affordable compute access and AI capacity building.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
445 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1020 0 R
/E (Data protection laws are broadly legitimising in this cohort but internal governance capacity is the gap. The most difficult regulatory area is data governance and internal policies, not cross-border rules or localisation requirements. Startups need help building internal data governance systems, not just navigating external regulations.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
446 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1021 0 R
/E (Data sharing is widespread and inadequately governed. 69% of respondents have shared data. Among those who have, security concerns and lack of formal agreements are the top challenges. Standardised templates and data-sharing platforms could directly address this gap, and demand for both is high.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
447 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1022 0 R
/E (The consensus recommendation across all sectors and countries is open, standardised, regional data repositories. Every interview cohort regardless of sector, country, or stage identified inaccessible government or commercial data as their primary constraint and called for open data hubs as the single most impactful change.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
